#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e009f408-0948-583c-9f36-aa61e528c12c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨6,3,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309479} {:answer/id #uuid "d5418a96-3eea-52a4-99cc-cba6ec5608b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨3,9,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309480} {:answer/id #uuid "99a54632-3487-5f66-b6f7-4f9fe3d532de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨9,6,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309481} {:answer/id #uuid "154c79d3-6bc5-5eb8-9dc0-6d20092039a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨6,6,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309482} {:answer/id #uuid "cf84f720-68bf-5be5-8c1f-4f6989161d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf84f720-68bf-5be5-8c1f-4f6989161d6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309483} :answer-field/id #uuid "a533e508-2e7f-507e-9ebe-7c8ad24094f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b183e73c-612e-556a-a6fa-cdffff7f175f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129202" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $3x-2y-18=0$ and $2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n3x-0-18=0\\,⟹\\,x=6,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0-3z+18=0\\,⟹\\,z=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨6,0,6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y-18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+9j+6k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨6,0,6⟩+t⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6-6=0,$ and $b=⟨6,9,6⟩.$"}]}
